WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.403
Diverging-lens work is heavy because students have to look at one diagram and understand two different kinds of line behavior.

2
00:00:05.403 --> 00:00:10.806
The real refracted routes spread apart after the lens.

3
00:00:10.806 --> 00:00:16.209
They do not meet to form a real image.

4
00:00:16.209 --> 00:00:21.612
Yet dashed backward extensions of those routes can still point to a stable image position on the object side.

5
00:00:21.612 --> 00:00:27.015
That image is virtual.

6
00:00:27.015 --> 00:00:32.418
The lesson becomes much easier if the learner stops asking only where the lines are and starts asking what kind of line each one is.

7
00:00:32.418 --> 00:00:37.822
Real route, Guide Line, or dashed extension.

8
00:00:37.822 --> 00:00:43.225
That line-role discipline is the real foundation of this worked example.

9
00:00:43.225 --> 00:00:48.628
Keep this takeaway in view: Diverging-lens success depends on keeping actual routes and backward extensions in different mental slots.

10
00:00:48.628 --> 00:00:54.031
Real routes means spread apart.

11
00:00:54.031 --> 00:00:59.434
Actual refracted light leaves the diverging lens without crossing.

12
00:00:59.434 --> 00:01:04.837
Dashed extensions means point backward.

13
00:01:04.837 --> 00:01:10.240
Backward extensions reveal where the routes appear to come from.

14
00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:16.839
This diagram is useful because it makes the different line roles impossible to ignore.

15
00:01:16.839 --> 00:01:23.438
A route that arrives parallel to the axis leaves the lens spreading out as if it came from the near focus.

16
00:01:23.438 --> 00:01:30.037
A route through the optical center still travels straight in the thin-lens model.

17
00:01:30.037 --> 00:01:36.636
Those real routes do not cross on the far side.

18
00:01:36.636 --> 00:01:43.235
Instead, if extended backward with dashed construction lines, they seem to meet on the object side of the lens.

19
00:01:43.235 --> 00:01:49.834
That apparent meeting point is the virtual image.

20
00:01:49.834 --> 00:01:56.433
The whole lesson depends on understanding that those dashed lines are logical extensions, not real beams of light.

21
00:01:56.433 --> 00:02:03.032
Keep this takeaway in view: Diverging-lens diagrams become readable once the line roles are kept explicit.

22
00:02:03.032 --> 00:02:09.631
Parallel route rule: After the lens it appears to come from the near focus.

23
00:02:09.631 --> 00:02:16.230
Center route rule: Use it as an almost undeviated second route.

24
00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:22.760
Imagine hearing a sound that seems to come from behind a partition because of how the paths reach your ear, even though the real source is elsewhere.

25
00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:29.290
A diverging-lens virtual image is similar in the sense that the eye or the diagram traces the routes backward to an apparent origin.

26
00:02:29.290 --> 00:02:35.820
The light does not truly gather there.

27
00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:42.350
The image is where the routes appear to come from.

28
00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:48.880
This analogy helps because it loosens the demand that every image must be a place where real light physically piles up.

29
00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:55.410
Keep this takeaway in view: Virtual-image reasoning becomes easier when an apparent origin is allowed to differ from the actual path crossings.

30
00:02:55.410 --> 00:03:01.940
Apparent origin means seems to come from.

31
00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:08.470
The perceived source position can differ from the real path behavior.

32
00:03:08.470 --> 00:03:15.000
Actual routes means still spread.

33
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:21.530
Real light after a diverging lens continues outward rather than meeting.

34
00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:28.128
The first route rule says that a parallel route through a diverging lens leaves the lens as if it came from the near focus.

35
00:03:28.128 --> 00:03:34.727
The second route rule says the center route is treated as undeviated in the thin-lens model.

36
00:03:34.727 --> 00:03:41.325
The image-status rule says the usual diverging-lens image is virtual because the actual refracted routes do not meet.

37
00:03:41.325 --> 00:03:47.923
The line-role rule says dashed backward extensions are geometry tools, not real light.

38
00:03:47.923 --> 00:03:54.522
Put together, those four rules explain why the virtual image is upright, smaller, and on the same side of the lens as the object in the standard school cases.

39
00:03:54.522 --> 00:04:01.120
Keep this takeaway in view: Diverging-lens reasoning becomes stable when route rules and image-status rules are kept together.

40
00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:07.718
Image status: No real route crossing means the image cannot be projected onto a screen.

41
00:04:07.718 --> 00:04:14.317
Standard appearance: The usual diverging-lens image is upright and smaller.

42
00:04:14.317 --> 00:04:20.915
parallel route -> appears to come from the near focus.

43
00:04:20.915 --> 00:04:27.513
center route -> approximately undeviated.

44
00:04:27.513 --> 00:04:34.112
Do not call the dashed extensions real beams.

45
00:04:34.112 --> 00:04:40.710
They are construction lines that reveal the apparent source position.

46
00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:46.733
A useful pattern in school diverging-lens work is that the image is usually virtual, upright, and smaller.

47
00:04:46.733 --> 00:04:52.756
It appears between the lens and the near focus on the object side.

48
00:04:52.756 --> 00:04:58.779
This pattern is not random.

49
00:04:58.779 --> 00:05:04.802
It comes directly from the fact that the real routes spread after the lens and that their backward extensions point to an apparent source on the near side.

50
00:05:04.802 --> 00:05:10.825
Once the student sees that signature as the natural result of the route behavior, the lesson stops feeling like a bag of disconnected facts.

51
00:05:10.825 --> 00:05:16.848
Keep this takeaway in view: The standard diverging-lens image signature flows naturally from spread routes plus backward extensions.

52
00:05:16.848 --> 00:05:22.871
Status means virtual.

53
00:05:22.871 --> 00:05:28.894
No actual refracted routes meet at the image position.

54
00:05:28.894 --> 00:05:34.917
Orientation means upright.

55
00:05:34.917 --> 00:05:40.940
The image keeps the same vertical sense in the standard diagram cases.

56
00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:44.814
Try four checks.

57
00:05:44.814 --> 00:05:48.688
What happens to a parallel route after a diverging lens?

58
00:05:48.688 --> 00:05:52.562
It leaves as if it came from the near focus.

59
00:05:52.562 --> 00:05:56.436
What kind of meeting point gives the usual image?

60
00:05:56.436 --> 00:06:00.310
A meeting point of backward extensions, not of real routes.

61
00:06:00.310 --> 00:06:04.184
What kind of image is that?

62
00:06:04.184 --> 00:06:08.058
Virtual.

63
00:06:08.058 --> 00:06:11.932
And can it be formed on a screen?

64
00:06:11.932 --> 00:06:15.806
No, because the actual refracted routes do not gather there.

65
00:06:15.806 --> 00:06:19.680
Those four checks anchor the whole lesson.

66
00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:23.554
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: the diverging-lens diagram now has clear route rules and a clear image status.

67
00:06:23.554 --> 00:06:27.428
Parallel rule means near-focus appearance.

68
00:06:27.428 --> 00:06:31.302
Use the near focus as the apparent source direction.

69
00:06:31.302 --> 00:06:35.176
Line role means backward extension.

70
00:06:35.176 --> 00:06:39.050
The image location comes from construction lines, not from actual beam crossings.

71
00:06:39.050 --> 00:06:44.842
You are ready for the worked example now.

72
00:06:44.842 --> 00:06:50.635
Start by drawing or noticing the parallel route and the center route.

73
00:06:50.635 --> 00:06:56.427
Show how the parallel route leaves the lens as if it came from the near focus.

74
00:06:56.427 --> 00:07:02.219
Then keep the actual routes and the dashed backward extensions in different categories.

75
00:07:02.219 --> 00:07:08.012
Look for the apparent meeting point of the extensions on the object side, and only then name the image as virtual, upright, and usually smaller.

76
00:07:08.012 --> 00:07:13.804
If the question asks whether the image can be formed on a screen, return to the line roles.

77
00:07:13.804 --> 00:07:19.596
No real crossing means no screen image.

78
00:07:19.596 --> 00:07:25.388
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: diverging-lens diagrams now separate actual routes from ghost-image logic.

79
00:07:25.388 --> 00:07:31.181
Step 1: Draw the parallel route and the center route cleanly.

80
00:07:31.181 --> 00:07:36.973
Step 2: Keep the real spread routes visible before adding dashed extensions.

81
00:07:36.973 --> 00:07:42.765
draw the real diverging routes first.

82
00:07:42.765 --> 00:07:48.558
add dashed backward extensions second.

83
00:07:48.558 --> 00:07:54.350
The strongest diverging-lens answers separate route behavior from image interpretation instead of blending them together.
